 "capsule": "After Theodosius I's death in 395 formalized the division of the Roman Empire, its eastern half persisted as a distinct Greek-speaking state governed from Constantinople, retaining Roman law and administration and often taking the initiative against Sasanian Persia. Under Justinian I (527-565) it codified Roman law in the Corpus Juris Civilis and briefly reconquered North Africa, Italy, and part of Spain, while enduring plague, riots, and prolonged wars with Persia. By 633, on the eve of the Arab conquests, the empire had just emerged from Herakleios's exhausting victory over Sasanian Persia (628), its treasury and armies severely depleted.",
